  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe address the problem of minimizing the AC1 upper bound by employing an iterative optimization strategy that alternates between the original sequence and a dual sequence. The central idea is to leverage linear programming to enforce constraints on the autocorrelation structure, guiding the search toward sequences that reduce the maximum convolution value. This approach builds upon the parent strategy's core concepts of using linear programming and gradient-based updates, but introduces new initializations, termination conditions, and prioritized exploration of highly constrained subsequences. The key rationale is that the autocorrelation structure defines the innate behavior of the sequence, and systematically exploring alternatives while maintaining mathematical rigor can surpass the parent's solution.\n\nThe method involves:\n   - Using the parent sequence as an initialization point (length 320)\n   - Employing multi-capacity linear programs to enforce autocorrelation constraints\n   - Implementing convex combinations to combine sequences with prescribed ratios\n   - Using harmonic filters to separate low-frequency and high-frequency components\n   - Dynamically adjusting search intensities based on surrogate estimates\n   - Maintaining rigorous bounds on intermediate variables\n   - Prioritizing updates to low-frequency components first\n\nThese elements combine standard optimization techniques with problem-specific heuristics to systematically improve upon the parent solution.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe optimization operates on a non-negative sequence a = (a\u2081, a\u2082, ..., a\u2099) of some length n. The autocorrelation constraint is enforced through a linear program:\n\nmaximize(\u03c3(b))\nsubject to: (a \u2217 b)[k] \u2264 M, \u2200k\n           b_j \u2265 0, \u2200j\n\nwhere M is the maximum autocorrelation value of a, \u03c3(b) is the sum of b, and (a \u2217 b)[k] is the discrete convolution.\n\nFor a sequence a and step t \u2208 (0,1):\n  b = (1-t)\u22c5a + t\u22c5d\nwhere d is the solution to the LP.\n\nThe optimization proceeds iteratively, with updated sequences normalized to:\n  s = \u2211(a) \u2265 0.01 and \u220a[0,1000]\nfollowed by reevaluation using the authoritative evaluate_sequence.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nFor the initial state:\n  - Copy the parent sequence (height_sequence_1) if available, else start with all ones\n  - Set the search direction vector to the initial sequence\n  - Normalize so that \u2211(a) \u2248 1 and all elements a_i \u2208 [0, 1000]\n\nThe iterative refinement follows these stages:\n  A. Linear Program (LP) stage:\n     1. Compute autocorrelation bounds: M = max_k (\u2211(a \u2217 a)[k])\n     2. Formulate the LP with:\n        Obj: maximize(\u03c3(b))\n        Constraints: \n          (a \u2217 b)[k] \u2264 M for all k\n          b_j \u2265 0 for all j\n     3. Solve using CVXOPT's highs simplex method\n     4. Project the solution b to feasible values\n     5. Update the working sequence via: c_new = (1-t)\u22c5current + t\u22c5b\n\n  B. Frequency analysis stage:\n     1. Apply FFT to decompose into frequency components\n     2. Identify dominant modes above a frequency cutoff\n     3. Modify low-frequency harmonics with bounded gradient descent\n     4. Filter out high-frequency noise components\n\n  C. Step size control:\n     1. Use autotuning based on singular values of Jacobian\n     2. Prescribe maximum allowed steps based on stability threshold\n     3. Preserve non-negativity with penalty terms\n\n  D. Multi-scale refinement:\n     1. Initialize with parent sequence interpolation\n     2. alternate between coarse and fine particles \n     3. Maintain budget allocation: 40% for LP, 30% for gradient, 20% validation, 10% archive\n\n  E. Feasibility assurance:\n     - After updates, re-normalize: \u2211(c)/diag_max(c) = s/1000\n     - Evaluate using evaluate_sequence with n=320 (fallback)\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nThe optimization uses the following variations by default:\n\n- Initialization:\n  - Base localization: Parent sequence (320) interpolation\n  - Perturbation: Add harmonic noise at fundamental frequency\n  - Diversity: Multi-start with seeds 23, 42, 65 (default)\n\n- Feature engineering:\n  - Scaling variants: Step-function, triangle, sinc approximations\n  - Projection methods: Linear, quadratic, phase alignment\n  - Regularization: L1, L2, non-negativity, frequency dominance\n\n- Parameter configuration:\n  - Primary: \u03b7 = 0.4 (energy dispersion), \u03c4 = 0.6 (autocorrelation cutoff)\n  - Secondary: \u03bd \u2208 [0.85,0.93] (gradient steps), \u03c1 = 0.2 (step rejection)\n  - Safety: Initial step < 0.05, \u220a = 1e-7 (non-negativity gap)\n\n- Special updates:\n  - Bassoon effect: L-shaped decay in high components\n  - Wave preserving: Preserve wave peaks above asymptotic level\n  - Constriction: Limit updates near singular points\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nThe algorithm estimates time requirements:\n\n- Primary compute: LP (dominant due to 2n-1 constraints) costs O(n\u00b2) operations\n- Secondary: Gradient descent on Fourier coefficients: O(1.3n log n)\n- Termination: Track costs at both iterations and direct evaluations\n\nFor sequences of length n\u2248512 (maximum n=1024):\n  - Core LP: 10-15 seconds with presolved simplex\n  - IEEE-754 double precision throughout\n\nRobustness handling includes:\n  - NaN/Inf detection: clip to [0,1000] upon detection\n  - Early termination: If n_max=1024,terminate upon 40 sec\n  - Budget management: Monitor wall clock for phases\n  - Frequency overflow: Limit kurtosis to < 2.5 standard deviation\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nImplementation must follow:\n  - Return list of floats with all x \u2265 0 and x \u2264 1000\n  - Update current best candidate only through exact evaluation\n  - Maintain L1 fallback: [0.1,0.1,...,0.1] if sum<0.01\n  - Authoritative evaluation in blocks of 16 evals (expensive)  \n  - Apply clipping after all modifications but before final validation\n\nThe method ensures that the returned sequence is non-negative, preserves application-defined norms, respects element-wise bounds, and is optimized against the given evaluation criteria.",
